{% comment %}
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  PARKED — the tile-reveal template.

  Not in use. Kept verbatim so the mechanic can be restored without rebuilding
  it. To bring it back: set REVEAL_ENABLED = true in flatirons/scene.js, put
  the `tiles` array back into buildMarkupResponse, and paste this into TRMNL's
  markup editor in place of template.liquid.

  Why it was parked: over a composed naturalistic photograph the grid read as
  a render fault rather than as anticipation — a broken image, not a picture
  arriving. Time-of-day and weather now supply the day-to-day variation the
  reveal was invented for, so the mechanic was costing the photograph and
  returning something we already had.

  See DECISIONS.md #11.
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %}
  Get Outside · Flatirons — paste this whole file into TRMNL's markup editor.

  Variables come from the polling endpoint:
    plate_url  message  weekday  revealed_count  plate
    tiles      12 items, each { index, revealed }

  Nesting is fixed by the framework:
    screen -> view view--full -> layout, with title_bar a SIBLING of layout.
{% endcomment %}

<div class="screen">
  <div class="view view--full">

    <div class="layout">
      <div class="scene">

        {% comment %} No "image-dither" class: the plate is already dithered
        to 8 tones, and dithering it again beats one pattern against another
        and produces moire. {% endcomment %}
        <img class="scene__plate"
             src="{{ plate_url }}"
             alt="The Boulder Flatirons at {{ plate }}, {{ revealed_count }} of 12 tiles uncovered" />

        <div class="scene__tiles">
          {% for tile in tiles %}<div class="tile{% unless tile.revealed %} tile--hidden{% endunless %}"></div>{% endfor %}
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="title_bar">
      <span class="title">{{ message }}</span>
      <span class="instance">{{ weekday }}</span>
    </div>

  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* Fill whatever the framework gives us, rather than hardcoding a height.
     The framework sizes the layout itself:

       layout height = screen-h - (gap * 2) - title-bar-height

     On an 800x480 device that is 480 - 20 - 40 = 420px, and the title bar is
     40px tall — NOT the 48 assumed earlier. Hardcoding 432px overflowed the
     layout by 12px and pushed the title bar off the bottom of the screen.

     Percentages instead of pixels means this stays correct if the gap, the
     bleed margin setting, or the device changes. */
  .layout {
    padding: 0;
  }

  .scene {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .scene__plate {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* cover, not fill: crop a sliver rather than stretch the mountain if the
       plate's aspect doesn't exactly match the container. */
    object-fit: cover;
    image-rendering: pixelated;   /* never smooth the dither */
  }

  /* Grid rather than 12 hand-positioned boxes: it guarantees the covers tile
     edge to edge with no rounding gaps, at any container size. */
  .scene__tiles {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: repeat(3, 1fr);
  }

  /* Revealed: draw nothing, let the photograph through. */
  .tile { background: transparent; }

  /* Unrevealed: a 25% ink stipple over white.

     The original ~12% white-over-image ghost was invisible on the panel.
     Reflective e-ink has no backlight, so pale greys collapse toward white
     and simply have nowhere to live. A PATTERN survives because it is built
     from full-black pixels with gaps — the panel renders each at full
     strength and the eye averages them into tone. Same reason the whole
     plugin is dithered rather than grey.

     TWO KNOBS to tune on the device:
       background-size  4px = one 2px dot per 4px cell (25% ink).
                        Larger = coarser and lighter. 6px is noticeably softer.
       opacity          1 = fully covered. Drop to 0.85 to let a ghost of the
                        photograph back through. */
  .tile--hidden {
    background-color: #fff;
    background-image: repeating-conic-gradient(#000 0% 25%, #fff 0% 100%);
    background-size: 4px 4px;
    opacity: 0.94;
  }
</style>
